WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
async and await make asynchronous code easier to write and read, but they do not remove concurrency concerns.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:17.560
An async method can still access shared state, start multiple operations, observe stale values, leave work running after a caller has moved on, or fail in a way that no one observes.

3
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.780
The control flow is clearer, not automatically safe.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.340
Avoid blocking on asynchronous work with synchronous waiting patterns in application code.

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:33.880
Blocking can waste thread pool threads, reduce request throughput, and in some synchronization contexts contribute to deadlocks.

6
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:40.700
It can also make exception handling harder to understand because asynchronous failures may be wrapped or observed in unexpected places.

7
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.420
Prefer async all the way through call chains where possible.

8
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:55.000
If a controller, service, repository, and HTTP client all support asynchronous operations, keep the chain asynchronous instead of blocking in the middle.

9
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.980
This gives the runtime more room to use threads efficiently while waiting for I/O.

10
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.980
Understand synchronization context in UI applications and legacy contexts.

11
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:13.400
A continuation may need to return to a particular context, and blocking that context while waiting for the continuation can create a deadlock.

12
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.940
Server-side code has different context behavior, but blocking still harms scalability and clarity.

13
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.940
Exceptions from asynchronous operations must be observed and handled intentionally.

14
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.720
Awaited tasks surface exceptions to the awaiting code.

15
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:37.580
Unobserved background tasks can fail silently from the user's point of view unless the design includes lifecycle ownership, logging, cancellation, and recovery behavior.

16
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.380
Avoid fire-and-forget work unless there is a clear owner.

17
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:51.080
A request handler that starts work and then returns may lose cancellation, dependency scope, user context, error reporting, and retry control.

18
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:58.380
If work needs to outlive the request, use a deliberate background service or queue pattern with explicit ownership.

19
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.180
Do not hold synchronous locks across awaited operations.

20
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:08.360
The lock protects a synchronous critical section, while an await can pause and resume later.

21
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.580
Mixing those models can create contention, deadlock risk, and confusing ownership.

22
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.760
Shared state accessed from async code still needs safe ownership or appropriate synchronization.
